masses of people  and literally blasted their way through  those crowds after I had a couple of  first-hand descriptions of what happened  when the tanks and the armored troop  carriers came in then I could readily  see why the toll would be as high as  that it it needn't emit but if you're  going to go in and you're shooting  you've got an awful lot of targets to  shoot at by the way when did you have  your first discussion that this was a  book I mean you said that you didn't  start out thinking about oh well I tell  you I that came the day after I got back  to New York I I called my editor  Roger Donald and little brown and I said  I've got something to talk to you about  and he said I've got something to talk  to you about well it turned out we had  the same idea but Tienanmen diary he  didn't know I'd kept a diary but he  thought that a book on this subject a  quick book would be useful and I thought  suggest I've got it hath written he said  wonderful we're going to make that an  instant book so we've never tried that  before but we're gonna do it this time  because the interest is here we want the  book to come out while people are eager  to know exactly step-by-step what  happened so I said fine and I proceeded  then a to complete the book which I had  about half copied I completed the book  and wrote the the final chapter  explaining as best I could while I  thought it happened and all that  Carla progr and he came down from Boston  stopped off at Taconic and spent the day  editing something which I've never  handed an editor in my life this was the  raw copy I hadn't had it I haven't had  it copied over I hadn't copy it over  myself it was full of interlining all  the rest of it he said that doesn't make  a difference I'm a good editor I can I  can read all through all that and he did  and at the end of the day he said fine  it's a book and I had answered two or  three questions and put little inserts  in he took it away turned it into their  copy reading department and I believe  the day after the fourth of July it was  turned over to the printer and we had  bound books by the 4th or 5th of August  and actual publication date was  September 1st  yes publication was September 1st and is  it too late have things changed since  you finished this or do you find this  still relevant I think every word of it  is very relevant there are some things  which I found out since which are not in  the book and I wish they were more  details of how the troops blasted their  way in a little bit on the political  background a few little facts here and  there but there is nothing wrong in the  book the I think it has the the right  mood it has the right explanation for  why the Chinese acted as they did and I  think I have accurately portrayed the  response of the students and most  particularly of the people of Beijing  and at no time during this trip did you  find yourself out of Energy Department  someone out of energy we were able to  deal with all this jet lag and traveling  and all this kind of stuff I think when  you have something like this on your  mind and when you're when you're really  revved up about something you it gentle  eyes tends to vanish  I can't recall a chip in which I had  less problem with jet lag do you want to  go back to China yes very much so I I'm  working on a much bigger and broader  project than that you know and re what  I've been working on for the last three  years is a book on the dumb shopping  period in China essentially what has  happened in China since Mao died  when did he die he died in 76 in  September exactly 13 years ago this  month and he left China in a dreadful  state of chaos dong came in and he put  things together and he got China going  in a remarkable way and he had ten  straight years in which he did he  changed son in many ways as it hadn't  been changed in thousands of years he  brought prosperity to the countryside by  eliminating the communes and enabling  farmers to go into private farming and  private to business and make money  very good for China and then this comes  along an unfortunate and contradictory  end really to his career as something  which damages enormous Lee his  achievements and it will take them a  long time to pick up on it all of that  goes in this book which I'm now writing  about a third of that book was written  before China and dari and I have I think  all the essential research for the other  segments had all been blocked out last  three years I spent an awful lot of time  in China working on this but I should  get back and take the temperature again  and make some political soundings so  that I have a little bit more certain of  the direction in which China is is going  to emerge after dung passes from the  scene and hopefully get a little clearer  idea who his successor may be I'm not  sure this is accurate to tell me if I'm  miss reading it you got a sense that  going into this whole experience of you  being over there  that you thought the students were gonna  win you come out more pessimistic saying  it may be a long time you change your  view during this yes I did very much so  I thought that I thought from all I had  learned before going over there and  indeed what I learned after I got out it  got over there in the short period of  time  that I had it looked to me as all the  students were winning or at least their  ideas or winning they didn't necessarily  have to  that dialogue with the government they  might not have it but the government  which had the same general objectives  was going to be spurred and pushed along  in that direction by the student  movement and this I must say was the  view of my Chinese friends they all  believed that extraordinarily and beyond  their expectations this was what was  going to happen and they were almost  deliriously happy about it well as we  know I was wrong and they were wrong and  it all came to a bitter bloody end and  it's not going to be that way the next  round when it comes and I'm sure it's  going to come with dong his death is  going to be a struggle of a different  type it's going to be led I don't think  by students but by adults probably by  the people of Beijing I don't know who  their leaders will be they didn't have  any leaders in this in in the Ken and  min thing they were just all of them out  there but there are be leaders and the  army will not be one unit as it was  supporting dong but probably conflicting  generals and divisions may there may be  some civil war I don't know there may be  some splintering up of China at least  temporarily reversion I hope it doesn't  happen but some of the Chinese think  maybe a reversion to warlordism or  something of that kind out of it all I'm  sure there will come a new China which  will pick up the beat and continue to  march towards something better but it  isn't going to happen tomorrow and he's  going to happen two years from now I can  remember during the June the those days  that you write about a television  reporter standing in Tiananmen Square  saying China will never be the same  again yep  this thing before the military moved in  you think that the reporters were too  optimistic and do you think it ever will  be the same again well they are  absolutely right that China will never  be the same again but that isn't what  they were thinking about they were  thinking that China was going to be  the new China that would follow would be  in the model of what they saw in the  student movement and I remember very  well dan Rather saying that I remember  Betty Bob Lord the life of the  ex-ambassador saying where's the same  effect and I know many people many  China's scholars in this country and  particularly a lot of Chinese students  and younger Chinese saying that how can  so many people be so long well let me  give you another thing the Cultural  Revolution which lasted about ten years  and embraced probably undoubtedly the  worst atrocities that China has ever  seen and death of millions of people and  the disgrace and murder of some of our  top leaders when that was all over now  dead dung in power every Chinese I spoke  to of whatever generation whether it was  of dung generation or the new youngsters  just out of school said we've learned  our lesson there will never be anything  like the Cultural Revolution again they  were wrong because inherent in China and  men and in the lies the government is  telling are the same techniques which  were used in the Cultural Revolution how  can they how can people make these  mistakes I think they're made because  they miss read some essential ingredient  ingredients in human character they miss  read in particular what revolves around  power and the extent to which a man who  has power would use whatever means at  his disposal to hold that power even at  great cost  I see dung as having used the power of  the military and the force of the  military and the shooting down of his  own citizens as a visual and vivid  demonstration that he is still in charge  and he's able to run that country with  the help of the army in spite of  anything people say he's not alone in  that use of power to hold power I think  that one has to under  that anyone who has absolute power as it  tends to be true in a dictatorship like  China or the Soviet Union will in the  end use that power use the power of hill  to keep his personal power he identifies  with his country and we must remember  that here's what the book looks like  it's both in paperback and in hardback  Little Brown publishes it 10 amend I  read 13 days in June by Harrison he  Salisbury our guest for the last hour  thank you very much thank you
 

